% ============================================================================
% dolch-sight-word-level — which of Dolch's five grade-banded difficulty
% levels a common high-frequency "sight word" belongs to (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE). A K-2 literacy library, grounding ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1's
% "phonics, decoding" Major Gap with a THIRD fresh-WebFetch literacy angle
% for this loop's `wave2-k8-literacy-foundations` item — genuinely distinct
% in KIND from `digraph-sound.adj`/`diphthong-sound.adj` (this loop's first
% two), which are both phonics/decoding tables (letters -> the sound they
% spell). This table is a WHOLE-WORD-RECOGNITION vocabulary axis instead: a
% "sight word" is a high-frequency word a beginning reader is taught to
% recognize instantly, by SIGHT, rather than sound out letter by letter --
% the opposite reading strategy from phonics decoding, and a genuinely new
% literacy competency this stdlib has never tabled before.
% ============================================================================
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? dolch_sight_word_level(the, $Level)   % pre_primer
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `dolch_sight_word_level(word, level)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the level WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any word not one of these source-cited rows. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a level and recall every word shipped
% under it:
%
%     ? dolch_sight_word_level($W, pre_primer)  % all 40 Pre-Primer words
%
% WHAT A "SIGHT WORD" IS, AND WHY DOLCH: In 1936, educator Edward W. Dolch
% published a list of 220 common "service words" (articles, pronouns,
% prepositions, conjunctions, and common verbs -- the connective words that
% recur constantly in children's text but often cannot be sounded out with
% early phonics skills alone, e.g. "was", "said", "of"). Dolch grouped these
% 220 words into five reading-level bands: Pre-Primer, Primer, First Grade,
% Second Grade, and Third Grade -- a genuinely different organizing axis
% from every phonics table already shipped (`digraph-sound.adj` and
% `diphthong-sound.adj` both key a SPELLING to the SOUND it makes; this
% table keys a WHOLE WORD to the READING-LEVEL BAND it is first taught in).
%
% EXTENSION (round 2 on this file): the table originally shipped 25 words
% (the first five of each of Dolch's five levels). This round completes
% Pre-Primer to its FULL 40 words, re-fetching and re-parsing the SAME
% cited UFLI slide deck rather than a new source -- exactly the untried
% angle this loop's own tracking issue backlog flagged after the file first
% shipped ("a future round can extend any one level with its remaining
% words as a further instance of this same gap"). The other four levels
% (Primer/First Grade/Second Grade/Third Grade) still ship only their first
% five words each, unchanged -- a future round can complete any one of
% THOSE the same way. This table now carries 60 of the full 220 Dolch
% words: one COMPLETE level (Pre-Primer, 40/40) plus four still-partial
% levels (5 words each), mirroring how `wave2-k8-science-foundations` grows
% a single gap incrementally across several rounds rather than requiring
% every instance to close in one pass.
%
% DESIGN NOTE -- why the four non-Pre-Primer levels still carry only 5 of
% their words each: the SAME "representative subset" convention this
% stdlib already established in `nutrition/food-groups.adj` (which tables
% 4-5 "example foods" per USDA MyPlate group rather than MyPlate's full
% food list). Nothing about the subset choice is content-based editorial
% judgment -- it is always the first N words of a level exactly as the
% cited slide deck itself presents them, in the deck's own built-in order
% (see Provenance). Pre-Primer was chosen to complete first purely because
% it was the level already partially shipped with the lowest word count
% among the two smallest bands (Pre-Primer 40, First Grade 41, Third Grade
% 41, Second Grade 46, Primer 52) -- keeping this round's added-row count
% modest while still fully retiring one level's "partial subset" caveat.
%
% The 60 words shipped, banded by Dolch's own five reading levels:
%
%     level          words (deck's own listed order)
%     -------------  ------------------------------------------------------
%     pre_primer     the, to, and, a, i, you, it, in, said, for, up, look,
%                    is, go, we, little, down, can, see, not, one, my, me,
%                    big, come, blue, red, where, jump, away, here, help,
%                    make, yellow, two, play, run, find, three, funny
%                    (ALL 40 -- this level is now COMPLETE)
%     primer         he, was, that, she, on                    (5 of 52)
%     first_grade    of, his, had, him, her                    (5 of 41)
%     second_grade   would, very, your, its, around             (5 of 46)
%     third_grade    if, long, about, got, six                  (5 of 41)
%
% HONEST ABSTENTION -- a word that is a REAL Dolch word, but at a level not
% yet completed here (e.g. `they`, Primer's sixth word -- Primer still
% ships only its first five), is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall on it
% ABSTAINS rather than inventing a level for a word this table's own scoped
% subset does not (yet) carry — the same honest-narrowing discipline
% `food-groups.adj` already established for a real MyPlate food outside its
% own "example foods" subset. Pre-Primer itself no longer has this
% caveat -- every real Pre-Primer word now recalls correctly, since that
% level is complete. A word that is not a Dolch word at all (e.g.
% `elephant`) abstains for a different, stronger reason (it is outside the
% cited source entirely, not merely outside a level's currently-shipped
% subset).
%
% Provenance: UFLI's Virtual Teaching Resource Hub page "Irregular and High
% Frequency Words" (curl-fetched directly and confirmed byte-for-byte) states
% verbatim: "In 1936, E. W. Dolch identified the 220 most common words
% encountered in the text children read. This high-frequency word list
% includes many easily decodable words (e.g., it, get) and some words with
% irregular parts (e.g., was, said)." That page links UFLI's own "Dolch High
% Frequency Word List Slides" PowerPoint deck, re-downloaded directly from
% ufli.education.ufl.edu this round (the SAME .edu domain and source family
% `digraph-sound.adj`/`diphthong-sound.adj` already established at `trust
% authoritative`, and the SAME deck this file's original 25 rows already
% cited) and unzipped as raw OOXML. This round resolved the deck's true
% slide DISPLAY order via `ppt/presentation.xml`'s `<p:sldId>` list and its
% `ppt/_rels/presentation.xml.rels` relationship map (rather than assuming
% the zip's filename order, e.g. `slide1.xml`, `slide2.xml`, ...,
% `slide10.xml`, which is NOT display order) -- a more rigorous re-check
% than the original sourcing pass needed, since this round reads far deeper
% into the deck. The resolved 226-slide order is: one title slide, five
% level-DIVIDER slides (each a bare `<a:t>` run naming one Dolch level:
% "Pre-Primer", "Primer", "First Grade", "Second Grade", "Third Grade",
% confirmed byte-for-byte in the raw slide XML) and 220 individual word
% slides, each one word's own `<a:t>` text run, immediately following its
% level's divider slide in the deck's built-in order. Counting the word
% slides between each pair of divider slides gives exactly 40 (Pre-Primer),
% 52 (Primer), 41 (First Grade), 46 (Second Grade), and 41 (Third Grade) --
% summing to exactly 220, independently reproducing the SAME per-level
% counts this file's original header already documented, confirming this
% round's re-parse is consistent with the original sourcing pass and that
% the deck faithfully reproduces Dolch's own well-documented 220-word
% count. The 40 Pre-Primer rows below transcribe every one of the 40
% word-slide `<a:t>` runs immediately following the "Pre-Primer" divider
% slide, in that exact deck order (the source's own capitalized "I" is
% lowercased to `i`, matching this table's existing atom-naming convention
% for every other row). The 20 rows for the remaining four levels are
% unchanged from the original sourcing pass.
% `https://ufli.education.ufl.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Dolch-Words-Slides.pptx`
% `trust authoritative` — UFLI is a university literacy research center
% (University of Florida, .edu). Nothing here is asserted from memory.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table dolch_sight_word_level {
    columns word, level

    row (the, pre_primer)
    row (to, pre_primer)
    row (and, pre_primer)
    row (a, pre_primer)
    row (i, pre_primer)
    row (you, pre_primer)
    row (it, pre_primer)
    row (in, pre_primer)
    row (said, pre_primer)
    row (for, pre_primer)
    row (up, pre_primer)
    row (look, pre_primer)
    row (is, pre_primer)
    row (go, pre_primer)
    row (we, pre_primer)
    row (little, pre_primer)
    row (down, pre_primer)
    row (can, pre_primer)
    row (see, pre_primer)
    row (not, pre_primer)
    row (one, pre_primer)
    row (my, pre_primer)
    row (me, pre_primer)
    row (big, pre_primer)
    row (come, pre_primer)
    row (blue, pre_primer)
    row (red, pre_primer)
    row (where, pre_primer)
    row (jump, pre_primer)
    row (away, pre_primer)
    row (here, pre_primer)
    row (help, pre_primer)
    row (make, pre_primer)
    row (yellow, pre_primer)
    row (two, pre_primer)
    row (play, pre_primer)
    row (run, pre_primer)
    row (find, pre_primer)
    row (three, pre_primer)
    row (funny, pre_primer)

    row (he, primer)
    row (was, primer)
    row (that, primer)
    row (she, primer)
    row (on, primer)

    row (of, first_grade)
    row (his, first_grade)
    row (had, first_grade)
    row (him, first_grade)
    row (her, first_grade)

    row (would, second_grade)
    row (very, second_grade)
    row (your, second_grade)
    row (its, second_grade)
    row (around, second_grade)

    row (if, third_grade)
    row (long, third_grade)
    row (about, third_grade)
    row (got, third_grade)
    row (six, third_grade)

    source "In 1936, E. W. Dolch identified the 220 most common words encountered in the text children read. This high-frequency word list includes many easily decodable words (e.g., it, get) and some words with irregular parts (e.g., was, said)."
    locator "https://ufli.education.ufl.edu/resources/teaching-resources/instructional-activities/irregular-and-high-frequency-words/"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Pre-Primer (all 40, complete): the, to, and, a, i, you, it, in, said, for, up, look, is, go, we, little, down, can, see, not, one, my, me, big, come, blue, red, where, jump, away, here, help, make, yellow, two, play, run, find, three, funny. Primer (first five of 52): he, was, that, she, on. First Grade (first five of 41): of, his, had, him, her. Second Grade (first five of 46): would, very, your, its, around. Third Grade (first five of 41): if, long, about, got, six. (Transcribed directly from the deck's own level-divider and word-slide text runs, in the deck's true display order resolved via presentation.xml's slide-ID list, not zip filename order.)" locator "https://ufli.education.ufl.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Dolch-Words-Slides.pptx"
}
